Time out unplaceable KubeRay worker pods sooner than placed ones that are starting.

The autoscaler moves an ALLOCATED instance to ALLOCATION_TIMEOUT once it stays
ALLOCATED for RAY_AUTOSCALER_RECONCILE_ALLOCATE_STATUS_TIMEOUT_S (an hour by
default), whether its pod can't be placed on any node or is placed and still
pulling its image or waiting for its node to boot. With several worker groups
ranked by priority, one timeout can't fit both: a short one drops pods that are
about to start, and a long one waits on a group that has no capacity before
trying the next one.

KubeRayProvider now marks a worker pod that has no node and either keeps
scheduling gates (such as a queue's admission gate) or is reported Unschedulable
by the scheduler. The reconciler times out an ALLOCATED instance whose pod still
has scheduling gates after RAY_AUTOSCALER_RECONCILE_UNSCHEDULABLE_TIMEOUT_S (two
minutes by default). A pod that is not gated but that the scheduler rejected may
only wait for a new node to boot and register its devices, such as the GPU device
plugin, so it times out after RAY_AUTOSCALER_RECONCILE_PROVISIONING_TIMEOUT_S
(ten minutes by default). Both are capped by the allocate timeout, and a pod
bound to a node keeps the allocate timeout.

Upstream status: not yet reported to ray-project/ray.

